=== 2. The private tiers are NOT reachable from the internet ===

    These addresses are RFC1918 and have no public NIC at all, so the
    attempt cannot even leave this machine correctly. That is the point:
    there is no address to attack.
       direct to app 10.20.1.2:8080 -> curl exit 28 (7=refused, 28=timeout)
[PASS] app tier unreachable directly from the internet
       direct to db 10.30.1.2:5432 -> curl exit 28 (7=refused, 28=timeout)
[PASS] db tier unreachable directly from the internet

    and SSH to the private tiers from here is equally impossible:
       nc app 10.20.1.2:22 from an unprivileged container -> exit=1
[PASS] app:22 not reachable from outside the VPC
       nc db 10.30.1.2:22 from an unprivileged container -> exit=1
[PASS] db:22 not reachable from outside the VPC

=== 3. The database accepts the app tier and nothing else ===

    from the APP tier (permitted: apps subnet -> dbs:5432):
       app -> db:5432 : REACHABLE
[PASS] the app tier can reach PostgreSQL

    from the PUBLIC tier (nginx). This is the peering non-transitivity
    test: public peers with apps, apps peers with dbs, and yet there is
    NO route from public to dbs. Not a firewall rule - no route exists.
       nginx -> db:5432 : BLOCKED
[PASS] the public tier CANNOT reach the database - peering is non-transitive

    the same from the bastion, which is also in the public tier:
       bastion -> db:5432 : BLOCKED
[PASS] the bastion cannot reach the database either

    control: the public tier CAN reach the app tier, so the failure above
    is specific to the dbs VPC and not a broken host:
       nginx -> app:8080 : REACHABLE
[PASS] public -> apps works, so the public->dbs failure is the peering topology

